From: EglantinePS5 <Vampinity@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: up: a present wait past its deadline goes on while the GPU side still moves (EDEN_UP_FRAME_WAIT_PROGRESS), and where a 3 s tick wait stuck (EDEN_UP_STALL_WHERE), off by default

What: on a first start the first 3D scene needs ~25 s on the graphics side; the present gave up at
4 s three times and the game ran ahead (the trigger of the 12 Nov first-start crash). With
EDEN_UP_FRAME_WAIT_PROGRESS on, WaitForFrame (nx2_gsv.h) also looks every 50 ms at the waited
fence, the split's back (GpuSplit::ConsumedNow) and a beat counter (new common/frame_wait_progress.h,
a relaxed +1 when a graphics or compute pipeline finishes building or fails, when the Vulkan worker
reaches a submit, when a texture decode finishes). At the deadline, if the fence is not reached and
something moved within the idle window, the wait goes on in 2 ms guest sleeps (as PaceToVblank;
a 2 ms host sleep if the guest sleep fails or returns at once) until the fence, no movement for the
idle window, the cap from the wait's start, or the emulation stopping. A wait that saw no movement
ends at the deadline as before. Every wait that ends at the deadline is covered (full wait,
frames-ahead previous-frame and fallback waits), not the unless_gpu_waits probe. The extra wait
runs after the wake lock is released.
EDEN_UP_STALL_WHERE=1 (vk_scheduler.cpp, log only, needs EDEN_NX2_HANG_WATCH=1): on a 3 s wait for a
tick that was flushed but not submitted, one line saying where the Vulkan worker is.

Switches (read once): EDEN_UP_FRAME_WAIT_PROGRESS: unset or 0 off; 1 count only (waits still end,
each says whether it would have gone on); 2 on with a 30 s cap; 5-300 on with that cap in seconds
(3 and 4 also mean 30). EDEN_UP_FRAME_WAIT_PROGRESS_IDLE_MS: no-movement window, default 8000,
500-60000. EDEN_UP_STALL_WHERE: 1 on, else off.
Log (Service): start "EDEN_UP_FRAME_WAIT_PROGRESS=30: a present wait past 4 s goes on while the GPU
side moves (no move for 8000 ms ends it), at most 30 s in all"; every 10 s (written by the next
wait) "EDEN_UP_FRAME_WAIT_PROGRESS: last 10 s: N present waits passed 4 s; A with no progress ended
as before, B would have gone on, C went on (D reached the fence, E ended by no progress, F by the 30
s cap, G by the emulation stopping); longest X ms"; per act (first 256) "... present #N fence (1,
V) not reached in 4 s (now V'); the GPU side moved X ms ago (fence +f, back +b words, j finished
jobs): waiting on, up to 30 s in all" then "... wait ended after T ms: the fence was reached|no
progress for the idle window|the cap|the emulation is stopping (now ...; ...; a GPU channel
waited|never waited on a semaphore during it)"; =1 "EDEN_UP_FRAME_WAIT_PROGRESS=1: present #N ...
it would keep waiting: ...". The old "not reached in 4 s" warning still comes only when the wait
really ends without the fence (text kept for the batch postprocess). STALL_WHERE (Render_Vulkan,
right after the "Hang watch: waited 3 s for GPU tick" line): "EDEN_UP_STALL_WHERE: tick T was
flushed (next to record T+1) but the Vulkan worker has not submitted it; worker at: <site>; N
chunks queued behind it (queue lock free|held)".

Merge with 0586 (both edit WaitForFrame): 0586 first. Its deadline line
"const auto deadline = start + std::chrono::milliseconds{TestFrameWaitMs()};" (and its comment line)
replaces the "{4000}" context line of draft.v2's third nx2_gsv.h hunk; no added or removed line of
either draft changed. So with EDEN_TEST_FRAME_WAIT_MS set too, the progress extension starts at the
aid's deadline (its lines still say "4 s"); 0586's give-up count and line come after the extended
wait. Do not combine the two switches in a test.

Not in TranslationSwitches (no shader change). The port scripts do not anchor on any changed line.

Cost when off: progress_wait is false (one cached value), the 4 s loop and the warning unchanged;
Beat() is one test of a value read once per pipeline build, submit and decode; STALL_WHERE one
cached static on the rare 3 s wait path. Behaviour byte for byte as before; machine code not.

Default flip (later, after the cold batch passes): common/frame_wait_progress.h
"inline constexpr long UnsetValue = 0;" -> 30 (=0 still off). That turns it on for every game; the
review suggests an Ocarina-only default for the alpha (one more check at the call site).
EDEN_UP_STALL_WHERE stays an off-by-default diagnostic.

Risks: a freeze can grow from 4 s to the cap when something keeps beating but the fence needs the
game; the present's service thread is held longer (other calls to that service wait behind it);
black screen with a frozen overlay for 15-25 s on a first start; the big texture lands only at a
frame tick, which a held present delays.

Origin and credit: our own work. Drafts drafts-2026-11-11/ports-7/first-start/frame-wait-progress/
draft.v2.diff (the reviewed version: idle 8000 ms, no single-core spin, semaphore note on the end
line, a failed pipeline build beats) then stall-where.diff, placed unchanged apart from the one
context line above.
